# Each layer's demand. One row per (filing, layer) whose demand is a three-point claim, stated or
# worked out.
#
# A derived demand is read worked out
#   A layer that files its demand `derived` files an output, and
#   composition/derived_quantities.sqlc works it out through its fusion. So this relation holds
#   that figure, and `d_derived` says it was worked out rather than filed. Every rule reading the
#   demand then examines a layer declared `derived` exactly as it examines one that states the
#   figure.
#
# One quantity of the resolved figures
#   composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc is every layer's demand, nameplate and draw, stated or
#   worked out, as two separate arms. This relation is its demand, kept to the rows with a figure,
#   and the restriction reaches each arm by itself. Reading the two through one outer join with a
#   filter on the joined side would plan every rule above this relation several levels deeper,
#   past what a plan read back at compile time can nest.
#
# A typed blank has no row
#   A layer whose demand is a typed blank has no row here: it has no entry rather than an entry of
#   zero. A derived demand that cannot be worked out is one of them.
#
# The three points are not an error bar
#   `low` and `high` are the edges somebody owns, and `mode` is the figure they would quote. Every
#   piece of arithmetic downstream that compares, sums or subtracts carries all three, because
#   reducing a range to its most likely value is the flattening this model exists to refuse. A
#   referral that only names a figure, as eliminations/derived.sqlc does, may read the most likely
#   value, and says so.
#
# What the width is made of is the claim's
#   `narrowsWhen` says what the width is made of: `instrument` means ignorance a better measurement
#   removes, and `intervention` means variation only changing the process removes. It is required
#   on every claim and lives with every claim, in epistemics/claims.sqlc, whose `owns` of
#   `pm:demand/pm:amount` is this demand. epistemics/widths.sqlc is the census.
-- composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc's pm:Layer/pm:Demand, where it has a figure.
SELECT q.filing, q.layer,
       q.low  AS d_low,
       q.mode AS d_mode,
       q.high AS d_high,
       q.unit AS d_unit,
       q.low = q.high AS is_a_point,
       q.derived AS d_derived
FROM (
    :compose(composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc)
) q
WHERE q.quantity = 'demand'
  AND q.low IS NOT NULL
